From: EglantinePS5 <noreply@eglantineps5.local>
Subject: [PATCH] EDEN_UP_JIT_COLD_CHUNKS=1: JIT slow paths go into 256 KiB cold chunks so hot code stays dense (PS5_Lichii 0202); off by default

Port of PS5_Lichii patch 0202 "JIT cold chunks" by Mihawk (mihawk-99/PS5_Lichii, commit 7aa138d9b,
patches/fritchii/fritchii-4012e52-ps5.d/0202-jit-cold-chunks.diff), GPL-3.0-or-later.

Upstream's finding: dynarmic writes a block's slow paths (deferred emits: memory-access
fallbacks, FP NaN and saturation paths) right after the block's hot code, so hot code is spread
over more cache lines and pages than it needs. On their PS5 lab, OOT's busiest guest cores ran JIT
code at an IPC of 0.64 with 44% of cycles waiting for instructions. With slow paths moved out,
upstream measured OOT at 33.1/33.2 -> 35.1/35.6 fps (ABBA, +6.7%), and on a Pro capped at 30 fps
cores 0-2 went from 67.0/65.1% to 61.1/61.4% busy.

With the switch on, the first block that has slow paths reserves a 256 KiB chunk at the hot
frontier; its slow paths and those of the blocks after it go into that chunk, and hot code
continues after it. When fewer than 8 KiB are left, the next chunk is reserved at the frontier. A
chunk is committed when it is reserved, so committed memory still grows in order. Hot code jumps to
its slow paths with rel32 (the emitters already use T_NEAR for those labels); slow paths jump back
to labels that are already placed. A block's registered size is its hot part only. With less than
2.25 MiB left in a cache, the rest of the block's slow paths stay inline, as without the switch.

Differences from upstream:
- EDEN_UP_JIT_COLD_CHUNKS, exact "1" (Common::PeSwitch), off by default; upstream is on by default
  with EDEN_JIT_COLD=0 to turn it off. Unset: the emitted code is the same byte for byte.
- Core only. Upstream forgets the chunk in headless/jit-permissions.inc's ClearCache; here
  EmitX64::ClearCache does it (every cache clear, ours in headless/dynarmic/jit_impl.inc and
  upstream's in a32/a64_interface.cpp, clears the emitter with the code), and BeginColdCode also
  drops a chunk that lies ahead of the frontier. BlockOfCode::ClearCache forgets it too, for builds
  without the port's replacement.
- Cold code is written behind the frontier, so it needs the separate writable view
  (headless/jit-alias.cmake). The call is compiled only with EDEN_JIT_ALIAS_LAYOUT, so host builds
  without that layout build and keep the old layout.
- No live override: our front has no EDEN_JIT_CLEAR_FILE, and a switch flipped mid-session would
  only change blocks compiled after it, so A/B it by boot.
- Log: "EDEN_UP_JIT_COLD_CHUNKS on=1 chunk_kib=256" once at the first block (on=0: no writable view,
  nothing changes), and "EDEN_UP_JIT_COLD_CHUNKS chunks=N" every 64 chunks over all caches.
- Fix (not in upstream): when a block's slow paths move to a new chunk, or back inline near the end
  of the cache, a jmp to the new place ends the old run. EmitDetectMisalignedVAddr's page-boundary
  check (on for every page-table access: detect_misaligned_access_via_page_table with
  only_detect_misalignment_via_page_table_on_page_boundary) falls through into the next deferred
  emit, the access's abort path; upstream's split leaves it running into unwritten chunk bytes on a
  misaligned access that crosses a page. 5 bytes per chunk.

With our JIT switches: the out-of-line lookups of EDEN_JIT_TABLE=1 and EDEN_JIT_PAGE_REUSE=1 are
deferred emits, so they move too (the play env has both on). EDEN_JIT_CACHE_MIB: chunks count
against the cache, and each full chunk leaves under 8 KiB unused (at most ~3% of the slow-path
bytes), so a cache fills that much sooner. Extra commit is that, one partly used chunk and 0.25 MiB
of lookahead per cache. EDEN_JIT_R13 and EDEN_JIT_DISPATCH_BITS are not
touched. Development PC sampling (eden_jit_block) maps a block's hot part only, so samples in slow
paths are not attributed to a block.

Checked on this Mac: applies on Eglantine 0ca2032 plus tools/eglantine-patches 0001-0510 (the
dynarmic and common parts), and every literal anchor in the front's CMake edits of block_of_code,
emit_x64 and a32/a64_emit_x64 matches as often as before. Not compiled, not run.

